  "member/unlocked-context.md": "# Member briefing\n\nThis content is only available after email verification.\n\n- **Priority claims line:** members can escalate water-damage claims within the first 24 hours.\n- **Policy review window:** annual coverage reviews can be booked with a dedicated advisor.\n- **Internal note:** mention code **KA-MEMBER** when transferring to the human team.\n\nKeep this information internal to verified member addresses.\n\n## Escalation matrix\n\nUse the fastest lane the situation allows. Never promise a callback window tighter than the matrix.\n\n| Severity | Example | First response | Callback window |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Critical | active water ingress, fire damage | escalate immediately | 2 hours |\n| High | uninhabitable room, heating outage in winter | priority claims line | same day |\n| Normal | appliance damage, cosmetic water stains | standard intake | 2 business days |\n| Low | documentation requests, policy questions | standard queue | 5 business days |\n\n## Priority handling rules\n\n1. Confirm the member's identity is already verified — never re-ask for the email on this page.\n2. Ask whether the damage is ongoing. Ongoing damage is always at least **High** severity.\n3. For Critical cases, offer the emergency contractor list *before* collecting paperwork.\n4. Record the severity you assigned; the human team audits these weekly.\n5. If the member disputes a severity, escalate one level and note the dispute — never argue.\n\n## Advisor booking\n\nMembers on the **Family Plus** and **Premium** plans can book a dedicated advisor for their\nannual coverage review. The booking reference always follows this shape:\n\n```\nADV-<memberId>-<year>\ne.g. ADV-MBR-284719-2026\n```\n\n> Advisors do not handle claims. If a member tries to book an advisor for an open claim,\n> route them to the priority claims line instead and explain the difference briefly.\n\n### What the advisor covers\n\n- Reassessing coverage limits after renovations or big purchases\n- Reviewing deductible levels against the member's claim history\n- Consolidating multiple policies into a family bundle\n- Flagging exclusions the member is likely to misunderstand\n\n### What the advisor does not cover\n\n- Open or disputed claims\n- Premium negotiations (route to retention)\n- Legal advice of any kind\n\n## Renewal talking points\n\nLong-standing members ask about renewals often. The honest framing:\n\nPremiums track regional risk and claim history, not loyalty alone. A member who has been with\nus since 2019 and never filed a claim will usually see the loyalty discount offset most of the\nregional adjustment, but not always — never promise a flat renewal.\n\nIf the member has filed more than two claims in the last three years, flag the conversation\nfor the retention team instead of discussing numbers yourself.\n\n## Data handling\n\nEverything on this page is member-restricted. Do not read identifiers, booking references, or\nthe escalation matrix aloud to unverified callers. When a session loses verification (sign-out,\nundo, new conversation), treat the member context as gone.\n\n",
  "policy/coverage-basics.md": "# Coverage basics\n\nUse this when explaining what a policy typically covers.\n\n- **Property damage** — damage to buildings, fixtures, and insured contents from sudden events.\n- **Liability** — harm the policyholder causes to others or their property.\n- **Additional living expenses** — temporary housing when the home is uninhabitable after a covered loss.\n\nAlways confirm the policy type and the exact event before stating that something is covered.\n\n## Covered events by policy type\n\n| Event | Basic | Family Plus | Premium |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Fire and smoke | yes | yes | yes |\n| Burst pipes | yes | yes | yes |\n| Storm and hail | yes | yes | yes |\n| Gradual leaks | no | after 2 years | yes |\n| Appliance breakdown | no | optional add-on | yes |\n| Garden structures | no | up to €5,000 | up to €20,000 |\n\nThe table is a *starting point* — riders and regional terms can change every row. When the\ncustomer names an event that is not in the table, say so plainly instead of guessing.\n\n## How to explain a coverage decision\n\n1. Name the policy type first, so the customer knows which column applies.\n2. State whether the *event category* is covered, before going into their specific case.\n3. Mention the deductible only after the coverage question is settled — mixing the two\n   confuses people.\n4. If an exclusion applies, point to the exclusion by name and offer the exclusions page for\n   details.\n\n> A useful phrasing: \"On Family Plus, burst pipes are covered. Whether this specific damage\n> qualifies depends on how the leak started — that's what the assessor will confirm.\"\n\n## Common misunderstandings\n\n- **\"Water damage is covered\" is too broad.** Sudden pipe bursts usually are; gradual leaks,\n  groundwater, and flood are three different categories with three different answers.\n- **Contents versus building.** A damaged floor is building coverage; a damaged sofa is\n  contents. Many customers hold only one of the two.\n- **Market value versus replacement value.** Most policies replace at today's replacement\n  cost, not the purchase price — this cuts both ways and surprises people in both directions.\n- **Liability is not personal accident.** Injuring yourself is not a liability claim, however\n  the injury happened.\n\n## When not to answer\n\nRoute to a human advisor instead of improvising when:\n\n- the customer asks for a *binding* coverage confirmation in writing,\n- two policies might overlap (e.g. landlord and tenant both claim the same damage),\n- the damage involves a third party who is also insured with us,\n- or the customer disputes an assessor's decision.\n\nFor everything else, answer from this page and offer the deductibles or exclusions pages for\nthe follow-up questions.\n",
  "property-damage/intake-checklist.md": "# Property damage intake checklist\n\nBefore submitting a claim intake, confirm:\n\n1. Everyone is safe and emergencies are handled.\n2. The customer can describe **what** was damaged and **when** it happened.\n3. You have **asked** whether photos, receipts, or reference numbers are available. Having them is optional; asking is not — never submit without having asked.\n\nKeep the intake concise: name, email, and a short damage description are enough for the first submission.\n",
